    "content": "Measles symptoms typically begin 7–14 days after exposure with a high fever, cough, runny nose, red/watery eyes, and tiny white spots (Koplik spots) inside the mouth. A characteristic red, blotchy rash appears 3–5 days after initial symptoms, starting at the hairline and spreading downward. Key Symptoms and Progression: Initial Symptoms (The \"3 Cs\"): Cough, Coryza (runny nose), and Conjunctivitis (red, watery, or sore eyes). High Fever: Often spikes to over 104°F (40°C) when the rash appears. Koplik Spots: Small grayish-white spots may appear on the inner cheeks 2-3 days into the illness. Rash: Flat red spots that may merge as they spread from the face/neck down to the body. On darker skin, the rash may appear brown or purplish. Contagious Period: Infected individuals can spread the virus for about 4 days before the rash appears until 4 days after. When to Seek Care:If you suspect measles, isolate immediately and call a healthcare provider before visiting to avoid exposing others. Seek emergency care for severe issues like difficulty breathing or seizures. Potential Complications:Measles can lead to severe issues, including: Ear Infections: Occur in about 1 in 10 children. Diarrhea: Reported in less than 1 in 10 people. Pneumonia: A common severe infection. Encephalitis: Swelling of the brain, occurring in about 1 in 1,000 cases, which can cause permanent damage. Note: The best protection against measles is the MMR vaccine.",
